Arma host packaging for Ubuntu Resolute
======================================

Prepare sources on a networked Resolute machine with Git, Python >= 3.12,
GNU tar, xz, and the distro cargo, rustc and matching rust-src packages.
Use /usr/bin/cargo and /usr/bin/rustc on PATH rather than rustup shims:
upstream rust-toolchain.toml pins a development nightly. From this checkout:

    python3 debian/make-orig-source.py ../arma-deb-source
    cd ../arma-deb-source/arma-0.1.0
    dpkg-buildpackage -S -us -uc

The helper exports committed HEAD (excluding debian/), runs cargo vendor
--locked --versioned-dirs with RUSTC_BOOTSTRAP=1 for artifact dependencies,
and creates arma_0.1.0.orig.tar.xz plus a build tree with the checkout's
debian/ copied in. Use a fresh output directory. Commit upstream changes
before preparing sources; debian/ may be edited without regenerating the
orig tarball. Reuse the same orig tarball for subsequent Debian revisions
of this upstream version, provided the rust-src dependency lock is unchanged.

Vendoring also uses --sync with the installed rust-src library/Cargo.toml.
This includes dependencies needed to resolve Cargo's build-std workspace,
even though the binary build only compiles core and compiler_builtins.
.cargo/rust-src.lock records that workspace's lockfile. debian/rules checks
it against the installed rust-src before building. If the Rust source lock
changes, regenerate the orig tarball and use a new upstream source version
before publishing; never silently replace an already published orig tarball.
Keep rustc and rust-src from the same distro toolchain installed together.

The orig tarball contains vendor/, Cargo's complete .cargo/config.toml
(including the pmi, devtree and burrow Git source redirects), bindeps and
offline settings, and generated LICENSE.dependencies. No vendor sources
are committed to Git. Archive ordering, timestamps, owners and modes are
normalized. Keep upstream Cargo.lock unchanged; registry checksums and Git
revisions come from it and the Rust library lockfile. Debian does not use
the RPM-specific packaging/cargo-vendor-git.toml fragment.

After installing Build-Depends, binary builds require no network, rustup,
prebuilt bare-metal libraries or warm Cargo cache:

    dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc

For a clean Resolute build with a locally configured sbuild chroot:

    sbuild --dist=resolute ../arma_0.1.0-1.dsc

Bare-metal libraries and linker
-------------------------------

Resolute has no prebuilt *-unknown-none Rust library packages. Native amd64
builds need x86_64-unknown-none; native arm64 builds need aarch64-unknown-none.
During every binary build debian/rules first runs Cargo -Z build-std=core
for tatu with --offline --locked, compiling core and compiler_builtins from
the installed rust-src. It copies those rlibs into debian/none-sysroot and
passes --sysroot only through the selected bare-metal target's RUSTFLAGS.
The subsequent host build compiles tatu as arma's artifact dependency and
embeds it in /usr/bin/arma, using the private sysroot for tatu and the distro
Linux standard library for arma. Nothing is written into /usr or rust-src.
Global RUSTFLAGS and CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS are unset because Cargo gives
them precedence over the target-specific sysroot flags. Distro Rust flags
from dpkg-buildflags are applied only to the Linux host target instead.
Ubuntu's lld package supplies ld.lld, selected for the bare-metal target.
No clang or compiler-rt C sources are needed by this core-only build.
The distro Rust source copyright notices are installed as copyright.rust-src.

Both Cargo invocations use a private, initially empty debian/cargo-home,
CARGO_NET_OFFLINE=true and --offline --locked. RUSTC_BOOTSTRAP=1 enables
Cargo's unstable bindeps and build-std support on distro Rust. This is
required by tatu's artifact dependency; it does not download a nightly.
The bootstrap and private sysroot are removed by the package clean target.
dh_clean excludes vendor/ to preserve checksummed Cargo.toml.orig files.
Package integration tests are disabled because they need burrow, kernels
and network access.

Cargo feature reference:
  https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/reference/unstable.html#build-std
  https://doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/reference/unstable.html#artifact-dependencies

Target archive: ppa:nmccallum/pichi, resolute only. These commands prepare
and build local packages; archive creation and uploads are handled by Infra.
